          "text": "{\"occurrence_id\":\"wharton-age-of-innocence-5363417821cd58b8\",\"core_classification\":{\"label_support\":{\"truth_conditional\":4,\"performative\":0,\"exclamatory_reflexive\":0,\"other\":0},\"confidence\":0.96,\"analysis\":\"May directly presents her love for Newland as a current emotional attitude, while specifying his artistic taste as its immediate occasion. The utterance is a light-hearted avowal or affectionate commendation, not a relational undertaking. Its emphasis and exclamation do not independently establish expressive/reflexive force.\",\"ambiguity\":\"The phrase may be playful praise rather than a solemn declaration of romantic love, but either reading remains predominantly truth-conditional.\"},\"other_diagnosis\":{\"tpe_failure\":null,\"core_not_context\":null},\"utterance_status\":{\"status\":\"direct\",\"description\":\"May directly addresses Newland during their conversation: “I do love you, Newland, for being so artistic!”\"},\"contextual_interpretation\":\"May uses the avowal while admiring Newland’s choice of her ring and light-heartedly changing the subject after their uncomfortable disagreement about convention. The wider exchange frames her language as conventional and somewhat superficial from Newland’s critical perspective, but that narrative irony does not displace the utterance’s core presentation of affection.\",\"evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"e1\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“I do love you, Newland, for being so artistic!”\",\"supports\":\"The first-person present-tense statement explicitly presents love for the addressee as true, strongly supporting T.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"e2\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"She “went on light-heartedly” and shifted to discussing Ellen’s admiration of her ring.\",\"supports\":\"The utterance functions as affectionate praise tied to Newland’s artistic taste and as a conversational pivot, without evidence of a commitment or undertaking.\",\"confidence\":0.97},{\"evidence_id\":\"e3\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The preceding narration depicts May as giving answers taught by “instinct and tradition,” while Newland experiences her response as conventional and childish.\",\"supports\":\"This supplies narrative irony and social scripting around the avowal, but does not change its core T force or create an unmodelled O force.\",\"confidence\":0.94},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The words are emphatic and exclamatory, but the narration provides no indication that May blurts them involuntarily or speaks before reflection.\",\"supports\":\"Supports E=0: emotional or emphatic delivery alone does not establish expressive/reflexive discharge.\",\"confidence\":0.93}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":false,\"familiarity\":\"none\",\"confidence\":null,\"contribution\":null},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"natural\",\"diagnosis\":\"T naturally captures the affectionate avowal; its light-hearted, conventional, and topic-closing functions are contextual nuances rather than missing dimensions of core force. There is no independent evidence for P, E, or O.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension\":null},\"notes\":null}"
